    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d907">

                <pb facs="1030" n="1018" xml:id="pg1018"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00114A Pacific War/350: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harrison</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bern</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-03-05">March 5,
                        1943</date>.<lb/>[Received March 5—9:23 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1517. American interests—Far East. Department’s 178, January 22. Legation’s 760,
                    February 2.<anchor n="11" xml:id="d907-f1"/> Swiss Legation, Tokyo, telegraphs:
                        <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“<hi rendition="#italic">Porte Parole</hi><anchor n="12" xml:id="d907-f2"
                            /> Japanese Foreign Office informed Gorgé: (1) According views Japanese
                            Government, strategic reasons against voyage <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Kanangoora</hi><anchor n="13" xml:id="d907-f3"/> still stand. (2)
                            Possibility other solution will be examined appropriate services
                            although simplest solution appears send medicaments, foodstuffs,
                            correspondence, et cetera next exchange.<anchor n="14" xml:id="d907-f4"
                            />
                        </p>
                        <p>Gorgé held extended interviews Japanese Foreign Office, examined all
                            practicable means early solution. Believes exchange vessel only
                            means.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harrison</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d907-f1" n="11" xml:id="d907-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported that the contents of Department’s telegram No. 178,
                    January 22, had been communicated to the Japanese Government on January 29
                    (740.00114A Pacific War/318).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d907-f2" n="12" xml:id="d907-f2-content">
                <p>Spokesman.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d907-f3" n="13" xml:id="d907-f3-content">
                <p>See telegram No. 2061, August 29, 1942, <ref target="frus1942v01#pg_817">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1942, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 817</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d907-f4" n="14" xml:id="d907-f4-content">
                <p>For correspondence on the second exchange of American and Japanese nationals, see
                        <ref target="#pg867">pp. 867</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
